th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/conditional-density-given-an-event.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2b-Probability-Distributions/conditional-density-given-an-event.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a continuous $Y$ and an event $A$ with $\\mathbb{P}(A)>0$, how is the conditional density $f_{Y\\mid A}$ defined?","options":["$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\mathbb{P}(Y\\le y\\mid A)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Y\\le y\\mid A)$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}y}\\mathbb{P}(Y\\le y)\\,\\big/\\,\\mathbb{P}(A)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Y=y\\mid A)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b1a53c8f59926b3e31a61563","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y$ and let $A$ be an event with $0<\\mathbb{P}(A)<1$. Which formula gives $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)$ through Bayes' rule?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)\\,f_Y(y)$","$f_Y(y)/\\mathbb{P}(A)$","$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)\\,f_Y(y)/\\mathbb{P}(A)$","$\\mathbb{P}(A)\\,f_Y(y)/\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4c0f1a442b162d75c02745ca","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y$, and let $B$ be a set, such as an interval, with $0<\\mathbb{P}(Y\\in B)<1$. Suppose $f_Y$ is nonconstant on $B$. After conditioning on $A=\\{Y\\in B\\}$, what happens to the density heights within $B$?","options":["They retain their original heights because only values outside $B$ are removed.","They increase by a common factor, preserving ratios between positive heights.","They become equal because conditioning makes retained values equally likely.","They increase by different factors, reversing the ordering of positive heights."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"47e96675d9446468212ee2d9","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition of $f_{Y\\mid A}$ require $\\mathbb{P}(A)>0$?","options":["Because events of probability zero are impossible","Because otherwise $f_{Y\\mid A}$ would exceed $1$","Because $Y$ must be independent of $A$","Because $\\mathbb{P}(Y\\le y\\mid A)$ divides by $\\mathbb{P}(A)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7e2c354e57ecfff31cbfc8e2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y(y)=2y$ on $(0,1)$ and zero elsewhere. An event $A$ is independent of $Y$ and has probability $\\tfrac{1}{4}$. Which is the conditional density on $(0,1)$, with zero elsewhere?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{2} y$","$8y$","$2y$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed44691d9ecb4978e54dbf19","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A waiting time $Y$ is uniform on $(0,4)$. Given $A=\\{Y>1\\}$, which density does $Y$ have?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{4}$ on $(1,4)$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$ on $(1,4)$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}$ on $(0,4)$","$\\tfrac{4}{3}$ on $(1,4)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"08795a35dba10a562cfade99","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $Y$ be uniform on $(0,8)$. An acceptance event $A$ has conditional probability $\\tfrac{1}{4}$ when $0<Y<4$ and $\\tfrac{3}{4}$ when $4<Y<8$. Given $\\mathbb{P}(A)=\\tfrac{1}{2}$, what are the conditional density heights on these two intervals, respectively?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{32}$ and $\\tfrac{3}{32}$","$\\tfrac{3}{16}$ and $\\tfrac{1}{16}$","$\\tfrac{1}{8}$ and $\\tfrac{1}{8}$","$\\tfrac{1}{16}$ and $\\tfrac{3}{16}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8cb63acf008cb7f6db42cadf","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A uniform variable on $(0,1)$ is known to exceed $\\tfrac{1}{4}$. Compute its conditional mean.","options":["$\\tfrac{5}{8}$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$\\tfrac{15}{32}$","$\\tfrac{2}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"321f2fe04116a605f33b0821","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y$ and let $0<\\mathbb{P}(A)<1$. Define $g(y)=\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)f_Y(y)$. Why does dividing $g$ by $\\mathbb{P}(A)$ produce a conditional density?","options":["Because $g$ has integral $1$, and division converts it into a conditional probability.","Because $g$ has maximum $\\mathbb{P}(A)$, and division gives maximum height $1$.","Because $g$ has integral $1/\\mathbb{P}(A)$, and division removes that excess mass.","Because $g$ has integral $\\mathbb{P}(A)$, and division gives total area $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"18be8992cdabe85df0485a15","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y(y)=\\lambda\\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda y}$ for $y>0$, where $\\lambda>0$, and let $A=\\{Y>s\\}$ for a fixed $s>0$. Which density does $Y$ have given $A$?","options":["$f_Y(y)$ for $y>s$, and $0$ otherwise","$f_Y(y-s)$ for $y>s$, and $0$ otherwise","$f_Y(y)$ for every $y>0$, unchanged","$\\mathbb{P}(Y>s)f_Y(y)$ for $y>s$, and $0$ otherwise"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"58a8b962b3a47d2253c3fbec","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y$, let $\\mathbb{P}(A)>0$, and suppose the compatibility factor $q(y)=\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)$ is nondecreasing in $y$. Which conclusion holds in general for every threshold $t$?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(Y>t\\mid A)\\ge\\mathbb{P}(Y>t)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Y>t\\mid A)>\\mathbb{P}(Y>t)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Y>t\\mid A)=\\mathbb{P}(Y>t)$","$\\mathbb{P}(Y>t\\mid A)\\le\\mathbb{P}(Y>t)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2d67a67f0dcf275c5f991fbe","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A lifetime $Y\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ is checked at an independent time $T\\sim\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$, and $A=\\{Y>T\\}$ is the event that it still works then. What is $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)$ for $y>0$?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{-y}(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-y})$","$2\\mathrm{e}^{-2y}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-y}$","$2\\mathrm{e}^{-y}(1-\\mathrm{e}^{-y})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b577e3678dd6047d760b430","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A lifetime $Y$ is uniform on $(0,10)$. Given survival past $4$, what is the density of the remaining life $R=Y-4$?","options":["$\\tfrac{1}{10}$ on $(0,10)$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$ on $(0,6)$","$\\tfrac{1}{10}$ on $(0,6)$","$\\tfrac{1}{6}$ on $(4,10)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a5fab0f041e833730fe23f1","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $Y$ have density $f_Y$, and let $p=\\mathbb{P}(A)$ satisfy $0<p<1$. On an interval $I$ of positive length where $f_Y>0$, suppose $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)=2f_Y(y)$. What must hold on $I$?","options":["$f_{Y\\mid A^c}(y)=\\dfrac{1-p}{1-2p}f_Y(y)$, and $p<\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$f_{Y\\mid A^c}(y)=\\dfrac{1-2p}{1-p}f_Y(y)$, and $p\\le\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$f_{Y\\mid A^c}(y)=\\dfrac{1-2p}{1-p}f_Y(y)$, and $p\\ge\\tfrac{1}{2}$","$f_{Y\\mid A^c}(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1-p}f_Y(y)$, for any $p$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6937291f81cd6d3696f6fe77","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"An $\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ lifetime is known to end between times $1$ and $2$. What is its conditional mean?","options":["$\\tfrac{3}{2}$","$2$","$\\dfrac{2\\mathrm{e}-3}{\\mathrm{e}-1}$","$2\\mathrm{e}^{-1}-3\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bd33da7ce8bf09cc1c4efb05","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A defect rate $Y$ is uniform on $(0,1)$. Conditional on $Y=y$, the defect indicators of two inspected items are independent Bernoulli variables with success probability $y$. Given $A=\\{\\text{at least one item is defective}\\}$, what is $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)$ on $(0,1)$?","options":["$2y-y^2$","$3y^2$","$2y$","$\\tfrac{3}{2}(2y-y^2)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6e4685ca074227bf29afb1e4","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2b_probability_distributions_conditional_density_given_an_event_c44fc4f3_017_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For an event $A$ with $0<\\mathbb{P}(A)<1$, the Bayes form gives $f_{Y\\mid A}(y)\\le f_Y(y)/\\mathbb{P}(A)$. Suppose equality holds, with $f_Y(y)>0$, at every $y$ in an interval $I$ of positive length. What does this say?","options":["$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)=1$ for $y\\in I$","$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)=\\mathbb{P}(A)$ for $y\\in I$","$\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid Y=y)=0$ for $y\\in I$","$\\mathbb{P}(A)=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bab83f69f1770adc3e17e7bf","time_limit":35}]}}